---
// The site's gallery as well as its table of contents: a walkthrough is the
// only place a figure comes with the commands that produced it, so the
// showcase is the list of walkthroughs rather than a separate wall of
// screenshots. Each card's image is one of that tutorial's own figures, cropped
// to 5:3 and re-encoded by astro:assets at build time (see lib/tutorials.ts).
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'

import JBrowseFigure from '../../components/JBrowseFigure.astro'
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import { f12CombinedCloseupUrl } from '../../lib/f12CombinedLinks'
import {
  brafV600,
  buildOnOpen,
  proteinLinked,
  tp53PhmmerRp15,
  tp53Protein3d,
  tp53R248,
  tp53UnirefBrowser,
} from '../../lib/jbrowseLinks'
import { tutorials } from '../../lib/tutorials.ts'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL
---

<Base
  title="Tutorials — JBrowseMSA"
  description="End-to-end walkthroughs that prepare the data outside the viewer: sequences to alignment to tree to annotations, ending on a link that opens the result."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Tutorials</h1>
    <p>
      The viewer draws an alignment, a tree and whatever annotations you give
      it. Producing those files takes the work, in command-line tools outside
      the browser. Each page below is one continuous line of work, where every
      step consumes what the step before produced, and ends on a URL that opens
      the result in JBrowseMSA. The picture on each card is a figure from that
      page, and the page has the commands that made it.
    </p>

    <ul class="tutorial-index">
      {tutorials.map(t => {
        const href = `${base}/tutorials/${t.slug}`
        return (
          <li>
            <a href={href} class="thumb-link">
              <Image
                src={t.thumb}
                alt={t.thumbAlt}
                width={640}
                height={384}
                fit="cover"
                position={t.thumbPosition}
                format="webp"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </a>
            <a href={href} class="tut-title">
              {t.title}
            </a>
            <p>{t.blurb}</p>
          </li>
        )
      })}
    </ul>

    <p>
      The <a href={`${base}/layers`}>layers reference</a> describes how values
      you computed (per-column values, labeled highlights, pairs of positions, a
      structure correspondence) load into the viewer as snapshot data, and the
      <a href={`${base}/examples`}>examples</a> page runs the viewer live, one
      page per usage pattern, with the source of each.
    </p>

    <h2 id="inside-jbrowse">Inside JBrowse</h2>
    <p>
      An alignment built the way these pages build it can also open
      <em>inside</em> JBrowse, through the
      <a href="https://github.com/GMOD/jbrowse-plugin-msaview">
        JBrowse 2 plugin
      </a>
      , connected to a genome view and, with
      <a href="https://github.com/GMOD/jbrowse-plugin-protein3d">protein3d</a>,
      the AlphaFold structure. Selecting a region in any view highlights the
      matching columns, codons and residues in the others. Each session below is
      one declarative URL.
    </p>

    <JBrowseFigure
      src="f12-combined-closeup.png"
      href={f12CombinedCloseupUrl}
      alt="A single JBrowse session at base resolution over F12 exon 3: the RefSeq gene annotation track on top and the react-msaview DNA alignment with its species tree below. The four cetaceans show a gap where every other mammal has a C, the shared single-base frameshift, and the highlighted column links the genome to the alignment"
      body="Genome ↔ alignment in one JBrowse session: the F12 locus (RefSeq annotation, top) over the react-msaview DNA alignment + species tree, zoomed to base resolution at exon 3. The highlighted column links the genome to the alignment. The four cetaceans carry a single-base deletion (a gap where every other mammal has a C) that disables the gene. The 14-exon model is registered to the alignment columns and lifted onto each species."
    />

    <JBrowseFigure
      src="genome-browser-tp53-protein3d.png"
      href={tp53Protein3d}
      alt="JBrowse: the TP53 gene with ClinVar, the p53 ortholog alignment, and the AlphaFold structure, all connected, with the nuclear export signal motif highlighted across genome, alignment, and 3D structure"
      body="Genome ↔ alignment ↔ 3D structure: the TP53 gene (RefSeq + ClinVar), the p53 ortholog alignment, and the AlphaFold structure as one connected session. It opens with the nuclear export signal motif (residues 339–350) highlighted on the genome, the alignment columns, and the folded structure."
    />

    <p>The same connected pattern without the structure view:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={proteinLinked} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          SRC ↗
        </a>{' '}
        — the Src-family kinase alignment beside the SRC gene.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={brafV600} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          BRAF V600E ↗
        </a>{' '}
        — the RAF family on the invariant V600 codon, with a ClinVar
        pathogenic-variant track.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={tp53R248} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          TP53 R248 ↗
        </a>{' '}
        — p53 across vertebrates on the R248 hotspot, with ClinVar.
      </li>
    </ul>

    {buildOnOpen.live && (
      <>
        <p>
          The links above carry their alignments as files. The two below carry a
          request instead, and the view builds the alignment when it opens: the
          spec names the transcript in <code>connectedTranscript</code> and the
          plugin looks its exon model up in the genome view's gene track, so the
          whole session is a URL short enough to type.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href={tp53UnirefBrowser} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              TP53 UniRef cluster ↗
            </a>{' '}
            — every reference-proteome protein in UniProtKB within 50% identity
            of p53, looked up from UniProt and aligned to the transcript in the
            browser. It submits no job to any service, so it opens in seconds.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={tp53PhmmerRp15} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              TP53 phmmer search ↗
            </a>{' '}
            — the same gene through a phmmer search of UniProt's representative
            proteomes at 15% similarity, the widest net EBI offers: p53's
            relatives across the tree of life. The wait is EBI's queue, from
            seconds to many minutes.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </>
    )}
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .tutorial-index {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 28px 0 32px;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(270px, 1fr));
    gap: 28px 24px;
  }
  .tutorial-index li {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .thumb-link {
    display: block;
  }
  .tutorial-index img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .tut-title {
    display: block;
    margin: 10px 0 4px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .tutorial-index p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
